ss signal: Johns Hopkins University Press / Johns Hopkins University Press"]},{"id":"scholarly","score":0.48,"confidence":"medium","evidence":["catalog text contains \"historians\"","academic press signal: Johns Hopkins University Press / Johns Hopkins University Press"]},{"id":"argument_driven","score":0.2,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"argues\""]},{"id":"synthesis","score":0.2,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"from\"","catalog text contains \"history of\""]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[{"name":"Baltimore","confidence":0.98},{"name":"United States","confidence":0.8}],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"The American working class emerged from the everyday struggles of low-wage workers, whose labor was indispensable to the early republic’s market revolution and economic transformation.","confidence":0.98},"academicOrientation":{"score":68,"confidence":0.9},"profileConfidence":0.95,"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"98b089b5013c45150eaa0e5c284f4ec720f6b965fc62271217ddc99bbfb8846b","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"summary":"\"Enslaved mariners, white seamstresses, Irish dockhands, free black domestic servants, and native-born street sweepers. All navigated the low-end labor market in post-revolutionary Baltimore. Seth Rockman considers this diverse workforce, exploring how race, sex, nativity, and legal status determined the economic opportunities and vulnerabilities of working families in the early republic. In the era of Frederick Douglass, Baltimore's distinctive economy featured many slaves who earned wages and white workers who performed backbreaking labor. By focusing his study on this boomtown, Rockman reassesses the roles of race and region and rewrites the history of class and capitalism in the United States during this time. Rockman describes the material experiences of low-wage workers -- how they found work, translated labor into food, fuel, and rent, and navigated underground economies and social welfare systems. He also explores what happened if they failed to find work or lost their jobs. Rockman argues that the American working class emerged from the everyday struggles of these low-wage workers. Their labor was indispensable to the early republic's market revolution, and it was central to the transformation of the United States into the wealthiest society in the Western world.","subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"African Americans","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-scraping-by-wage-labor-slavery-and-survival-in-early-baltimore-seth-rockman"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Capitalism","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-scraping-by-wage-labor-slavery-and-survival-in-early-baltimore-seth-rockman"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Economic conditions","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-scraping-by-wage-labor-slavery-and-survival-in-early-baltimore-seth-rockman"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Employment","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-scraping-by-wage-labor-slavery-and-survival-in-early-baltimore-seth-rockman"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"History","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-scraping-by-wage-labor-slavery-and-survival-in-early-baltimore-seth-rockman"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Labor","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-scraping-by-wage-labor-slavery-and-survival-in-early-baltimore-seth-rockman"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Race relations","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-scraping-by-wage-labor-slavery-and-survival-in-early-baltimore-seth-rockman"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Slavery","sourceI